<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Janissaries</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as janissary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Janissaries were the elite infantry corps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, originally recruited from Christian boys through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a> system. They became one of the first standing armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to use firearms on a large scale. Comparable slave-soldier corps were developed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, and the Janissaries were abolished in 1826.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janissaries.jpg" alt="Janissaries" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lambert Wyts · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lambert_Wyts_-_Agha_of_the_Janissaries_and_a_B%C3%B6l%C3%BCk_of_the_Janissaries.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Janissary Corps was formed in the 14th century, either under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> or under his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a>. The Ottoman government took a tax of one-fifth on all slaves taken in war, and from this pool of men the sultans built a personal army loyal only to themselves. From the 1380s to 1648 the corps was filled through the devşirme, a levy of boys, predominantly Balkan Christians, who were enslaved, circumcised by force and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The levy was abolished in 1648. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were never subject to it.</p><p>A boy was first sent to a Turkish family in the provinces to learn Turkish and the customs of Ottoman society, and was then trained in the acemi oğlan, or cadet, school in the capital, where the brightest were selected for the palace institution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enderun-school">Enderun</a>. Janissaries lived in strict discipline in nearly monastic conditions and were forbidden to marry until retirement, typically in their forties. The devşirme was deeply resented among Christians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and in Serbian, Greek and Bulgarian memory it became one of the most enduring symbols of Ottoman rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Janissaries were kapıkulları, door servants of the sultan, neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> nor ordinary slaves. They wore distinctive uniforms, lived in barracks, marched to the music of the mehter band and received regular salaries and pensions. The corps was divided into orta, or battalions, led by a commander called the ağa. Under Suleiman I there were 165 orta, rising to 196, in three sub-corps: the cemaat, the bölük and the sekban. A semi-autonomous corps was based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>.</p><p>In the classical period the Janissaries were only about a tenth of the Ottoman army, the provincial cavalry being the main force. They numbered about 1,000 in the 14th century and about 6,000 in 1475, and from the 1530s the corps expanded, to about 40,000 men by 1609. They began adopting firearms in the early to mid 1400s and in 1605 became one of the first European armies to use rotating lines of volley fire. They took part in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the late 16th century a sultan gave in to pressure and allowed Janissaries' sons to join, a practice forbidden for 200 years, and by the 17th century civilians bought their way in for the status it gave. Janissaries came to own land and businesses, mutinied, dictated policy and changed sultans by palace coups, and they resisted modernisation of the army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> abolished the corps in 1826 in the Auspicious Incident, in which 6,000 or more were executed. Janissary music influenced Mozart and Beethoven, and the mehter band was organised again in 1952 under the Istanbul Military Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Janissary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1473 Mehmed II marched east; after an initial Aq Qoyunlu success on the Euphrates, the Ottoman army, with its <b>janissaries</b> and field artillery, defeated Uzun Hasan decisively at the Battle of Otlukbeli on 11 August 1473.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its pre-eminence rested on the <b>janissary</b> corps, an elite infantry force recruited through the devshirme, whereby Christian boys, mainly from the Balkans, were periodically levied, converted to Islam, educated and trained as professional soldiers loyal to the sultan rather than to any tribal or feudal allegiance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In return he received the title of beylerbey, some 2,000 <b>Janissaries</b> and the right to recruit volunteers in Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>There Sultan Selim I deployed artillery and musket-armed <b>janissary</b> infantry against the Qizilbash cavalry, whose leaders, by tradition, scorned firearms as unmanly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Cautious reform under Selim III (r. 1789–1807) produced the Nizam-i Cedid, the New Order, but conservatives and the <b>Janissaries</b> revolted and, after a series of coups, installed Mahmud II.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Their standing infantry corps, the <b>Janissaries</b>, adopted firearms in the fifteenth century, and Ottoman artillery was among the most powerful in the world.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li></ul></section>
